(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-htn-beta-blockers-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Beta Blockers Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How do beta blockers relieve benign essential tremor?","choices":["Cardiac beta-1 blockade","Vascular alpha-1 blockade","Skeletal muscle beta-2 blockade","Central alpha-2 stimulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blocking beta-2 receptors on skeletal muscle is the mechanism given for benign essential tremor; panic attacks respond through sympathetic and central effects. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which effect on blood lipids is listed as an adverse effect of beta blockers?","choices":["Elevated triglycerides","Lowered triglycerides","Raised HDL cholesterol","Lowered LDL cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Elevated triglycerides are listed among beta blocker adverse effects; by contrast, prazosin mildly lowers triglycerides and raises HDL (high-density lipoprotein). — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Why does stopping a beta blocker abruptly provoke a rebound?","choices":["Catecholamine synthesis stops","Receptors upregulate during blockade","Renin release is permanently lost","The drug has a long half-life"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic blockade upregulates beta receptors, so sudden removal exposes more receptors to catecholamines; the drug is withdrawn slowly. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Where do beta blockers sit in the management of uncomplicated hypertension?","choices":["First line for most patients","First line in diabetes","Only for hypertensive crisis","Not recommended first line"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are not recommended for first-line management; first line is an ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker, or a dihydropyridine. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"For hypertension, which patient is a beta blocker expected to work best in?","choices":["An older patient with asthma","A patient with intermittent claudication","A young patient with resting tachycardia","A patient with frequent hypoglycemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They work best in young patients with resting tachycardia, raised catecholamines and raised renin, though fatigue and limited exercise are common complaints. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A patient on propranolol reports cold fingers and calf pain when walking. What is the mechanism?","choices":["Vascular alpha-1 blockade","Cardiac beta-1 stimulation","Calcium channel blockade","Vascular beta-2 blockade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blocking beta-2 receptors in blood vessels impairs peripheral circulation: cold extremities (Raynaud's phenomenon), muscle fatigue and intermittent claudication. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Why do beta blockers prolong hypoglycemia?","choices":["They inhibit glycogenolysis","They increase insulin secretion","They block glucose absorption","They activate hepatic glucokinase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low glucose normally triggers epinephrine-driven glycogenolysis; blocking it slows recovery and masks the symptoms. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A patient takes nadolol. How is it classified?","choices":["Non-selective beta blocker","Beta-1 selective blocker","Alpha-1 and beta blocker","Central alpha-2 agonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nadolol is one of the first-generation, non-selective agents, with propranolol, penbutolol, pindolol, sotalol and timolol. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A patient with mild asthma needs a beta blocker after a myocardial infarction. Which choice carries the lowest risk of bronchospasm?","choices":["Propranolol","Metoprolol","Nadolol","Timolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bronchospasm is less likely with beta-1 selective agents such as metoprolol, because pulmonary beta-2 receptors are relatively spared. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"When a non-selective beta blocker is first started, what happens to total peripheral resistance?","choices":["It falls immediately","It does not change","It falls, then rises","It rises by reflex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac output falls, so peripheral resistance rises reflexly at first; mean arterial pressure falls only with chronic therapy as the baroreflex adjusts. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is used for migraine prophylaxis?","choices":["Esmolol","Propranolol","Labetalol","Bisoprolol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Propranolol and timolol are used for migraine prophylaxis; the mechanism is uncertain (central or vascular), and low doses are used. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is used for glaucoma?","choices":["Timolol","Atenolol","Esmolol","Carvedilol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Timolol reduces production of aqueous humor in the ciliary body; systemic effects from the eye drops are notable. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Besides beta blockade, what additional action does labetalol have?","choices":["ACE inhibition","Alpha-1 blockade","Central alpha-2 stimulation","Potassium channel opening"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Labetalol is a third-generation agent that blocks alpha-1 receptors and has beta-2 agonist activity, giving direct vasodilation. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which is a listed indication for beta blockers?","choices":["Opiate withdrawal","Hypertensive crisis","Acute panic attacks","Prostatic hyperplasia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers help acute panic attacks through sympathetic and central effects; the list also includes glaucoma, migraine prophylaxis, thyrotoxicosis and essential tremor. — Antihypertensives.pptx, Slide 69"}]};
